            vectors/keri/ moves to keri_vectors_format 2 for the profile's draft 6 = decision:
              nid: 4tkkp50h
              why: >-
                vectors/keri/ moves to keri_vectors_format 2 for the profile's draft 6 (2026-09-24), under @8vwrexxc's rule that any change to the required behaviour is a bump. Added: mode-mismatch in both directions, built from legacy.json's KERIA material; unauthenticated, an unsigned 401 answering a canonical request; an unsigned 200 as missing-signature; a response signed by an AID other than the one the client expects, as unknown-key; a covered @authority outside the served set, as signature-mismatch; a base that cannot be built, as signature-mismatch; and draft 6 R4's key states -- the passcode-rotated Signify controller (keys [new, prior-next], threshold ["1","0"]) as an accept case, and "1" over two keys and 2-of-3 as unsupported-signer. The keys table gains an optional key_state (qb64 verfers and a threshold in keripy's form) and an effective_key that is null when the state has none; an implementation with a KERI stack builds the effective key from key_state itself, and fiki's driver, which has none, reads effective_key. Cases may carry a policy object that extends the file's, which is where the served authorities and the expected response keyid go. The two mode-mismatch cases are marked verified_by_fiki false: fiki cannot tell a legacy header from a malformed one without the legacy parser, so it carries those cases as data, exactly as it carries legacy.json. Each refusal now has one defect; the two cases that had two were split, and the duplicate-ignoring-parameter-order check, which cannot be shown with a single defect while req is the only supported parameter, stays a fiki unit test rather than a vector. The header-only body cases hand the verifier no body, so they test the headers rather than the read-time rule. One rule decides whether a keyid is a well-formed AID, stated in each file's header and shared by the generator and fiki's driver. requests.json says that its sha-512 digest cases are verify-only, since a sha-256 signer cannot reproduce them. Tradeoff: signify-ts and keripy must move to format 2 together, and fiki-py still verifies every case except the two mode-mismatch ones, so conformance claims against this set have to say which of those two an implementation checked itself.
            fiki publishes the KERI profile of RFC 9421, beside its vectors = decision:
              nid: 997vxdu7
              why: >-
                fiki hosts the KERI profile of RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures, at docs/keri-profile.md, as version 1 of 2026-09-24. Daniel approved publishing it here on 2026-09-24. The profile has to be public: keripy, KERIA and signify-ts implement it for WebOfTrust/keripy#1669 and keria#467, the vectors/keri/ files (@8vwrexxc) name it as their contract, and an implementer outside Bakobo cannot follow a pointer to a private working file. Chose fiki because the profile sits next to its vectors, so a change to the rules and the vectors that pin them can land in one commit and be reviewed together, and because fiki is Bakobo's to publish on its own schedule: it can land before any WebOfTrust PR does, whereas putting it in keripy would make its publication wait on a pull request to a repository Bakobo does not maintain. The published copy is a cleaned version of the working draft: review ids, private file references and the draft history are removed, and the normative content is unchanged. Rejected publishing it inside a keripy PR, for the timing reason above, and rejected a separate repository, which would put a second copy of the contract beside the vectors and let the two drift. Tradeoff: a KERI-specific document now lives in a library that is deliberately KERI-free (@07wstqk7). It is documentation and conformance data, never code fiki imports, and a reader may still take its presence as a claim that fiki implements the whole profile, which it does not (it has no legacy mode). The vectors' profile block points at this document by URL, which changes metadata only, so keri_vectors_format stays 2.
            The Rust port meets the KERI profile with a typed resolver and caller errors as a kind = decision:
              nid: 5e2phpjy
              why: >-
                The Rust port gains fiki-py's KERI-profile surface (@7f28p7xk, @2f227n4r, @7p9s3g9k, @6g9zjsv9) and runs every file under vectors/keri/ from the repository root, translating py's API rather than transliterating it; this node records each place the two shapes differ, so a reader comparing them is not left to guess which difference is deliberate. THE RESOLVER is a Resolver, an Arc<dyn Fn(&str) -> Result<Option<[u8; 32]>> + Send + Sync>, on VerifyOptions. The fixed-size array makes py's resolver-returned-the-wrong-length MalformedKey unrepresentable rather than checked; Option<..> carries py's None-means-UnknownKey; and the Result is how a resolver raises UnsupportedSigner or MalformedKey itself, which is why Error::new and Error::detailed become public. Rejected a resolver trait, which is more ceremony than one method needs when closures already implement Fn, and a borrowed &dyn Fn, which would put a lifetime on VerifyOptions and on every caller that names the type. CALLER ERRORS: where py raises ValueError or TypeError outside its FikiError taxonomy -- a minimum smaller than the profile's, expected_aid and a resolver together, a response covering "content-digest";req verified against a Request with no body -- the Rust port returns Err with a new Kind::InvalidArgument, which has no profile code and which the KERI driver's totality test exempts by name. Rejected panicking, the usual Rust answer to a broken contract, because the third case is reached from wire content: a peer chooses to cover "content-digest";req, so a panic there would let a remote party crash a verifier. Rejected a second error type, which would change every function's Result for a case few callers hit. expected_aid and resolve stay two Option fields rather than an enum, so existing VerifyOptions literals keep compiling. THE STATUS is a u16, so py's negative, boolean and string statuses are unrepresentable; 99 and 1000 are still refused as MissingComponent at run time. COMPONENTS stay strings, plain or in serialized form, with req() spelling a request component, and Verdict.covered uses py's spelling; the error's offending value stays the port's single detail field rather than py's named attributes. KERI_VECTORS_FORMAT is exported from the library beside VECTORS_FORMAT, where py keeps it in its driver, because this port already declares its shared format in the library and the two declarations belong together. NO NEW DEPENDENCY: the hand-rolled RFC 8941 subset (@2tt6fmc0) gains component parameters, tokens and decimals, so a header py's parser reads is classified the same way here rather than failing to parse, and CI's dependency budget is unchanged. Tradeoff: InvalidArgument is a twenty-fourth kind that only this port has, and a Rust caller matching on Kind must handle a variant no message can produce; and the resolver's Send + Sync bound refuses a closure over non-thread-safe state, which a caller wraps in a Mutex.
              children:
                The Rust port refuses small-order Ed25519 public keys as MalformedKey = decision:
                  nid: 2t8xctts
                  why: >-
                    The Rust port refuses a small-order Ed25519 public key as MalformedKey before any signature is checked, on every path a key reaches the verifier: an AID decoded by verifying_key (so expected_aid and a B keyid too), a raw keyid decoded inline, and a key a resolver returns. Tick 27eo records why: fiki-py, through OpenSSL, accepts a forged signature under the identity-point key -- public key 0x01 followed by 31 zero bytes and signature 0x01 followed by 63 zero bytes verify over any message -- and ed25519-dalek's non-strict verify, which this port uses, is believed to accept it the same way; a small-order key has no secret behind it that only one party holds, so a signature under it proves nothing about who sent the message. The conductor of the KERI-parity round directed on 2026-10-07 that every port refuse such keys fail-closed now rather than copy py, which is fixed separately. Chose ed25519-dalek's own VerifyingKey::is_weak, which tests the decompressed point for small order and so catches the eight small-order points under any encoding that decompresses, canonical or not, over a hand-written byte blocklist that would have to enumerate the non-canonical encodings itself. Chose to refuse rather than switch to verify_strict alone, because verify_strict reports a weak key as a failed signature, which is SignatureMismatch, and the conductor asked for MalformedKey ahead of the signature check, where section 9 puts key problems. Rejected leaving it to the resolver, which would make the guarantee depend on every caller's resolver. This changes one measured property the port's README states: verifying_key no longer accepts any 32 bytes, since the all-zero encoding is a point of order 4; any 32 bytes that decompress to a point of large order are still accepted and fail at verification. Tradeoff: a cross-port difference until every port lands the same refusal, which no shared vector pins until the next vectors_format bump adds one; and the Rust port now refuses messages fiki-py accepts, which is the intended direction.
                  children:
                    A public key is the canonical encoding of an on-curve point, or it is MalformedKey = decision:
                      nid: 34qlc8r3
                      why: >-
                        Extends @2t8xctts at the KERI-parity conductor's ruling of 2026-10-07, which extends D-C3BH after the C# port's check: any 32 bytes that are not the canonical encoding of an on-curve Ed25519 point are refused as MalformedKey, on the same paths and at the same point as a small-order key -- an AID or keyid as decoded, or a key a resolver returns, before any signature is checked. That covers a y coordinate with no point on the curve, a y at or above the field prime p (a second spelling of y minus p), and a set sign bit on a point whose x is zero; with @2t8xctts's small-order refusal it covers all fourteen small-order encodings, canonical or not. The Rust port tests y below p by bytes, since ed25519-dalek's decompression reduces y modulo p and so accepts the non-canonical spelling, and leaves the curve test to decompression and the small-order test to is_weak; x is zero only at y = 1 and y = -1, both small-order, so is_weak also refuses either with the sign bit set. fiki-py reports an off-curve key as SignatureMismatch, discovered only when it fails to verify; the conductor directs ports not to copy that, because a key that cannot be a key is the signer's identifier being malformed, which section 9 places before the signature. This REVISES a measured claim the port's README and tests/unit.rs made, that verifying_key accepts any 32 bytes and leaves the point to verification: it now refuses up front every encoding it cannot verify under. Tradeoff: a further difference from fiki-py until py lands the same rule, unpinned by any vector until the next vectors_format bump.
                The Rust port parses URLs and structured fields strictly and without normalizing = decision:
                  nid: 4pz4mcgq
                  why: >-
                    Several parsing choices in the Rust port change what it accepts or what it builds in ways neither vector set pins, so they are recorded here rather than left in commit messages, at the KERI-parity conductor's direction of 2026-10-07. URLS ARE SPLIT, NEVER NORMALIZED: @path is the path as the caller gives it, with no dot-segment removal, no percent-decoding and no re-encoding, and @query is the raw query, because RFC 9421 section 2.2.6 and 2.2.7 take the target as sent and a normalizing verifier computes a different base from the one signed; the one rewrite is section 2.2.3's for @authority, a lowercased host and a default port dropped. AN IPv6 LITERAL KEEPS ITS BRACKETS in @authority, as [::1] or [::1]:8443, because RFC 3986 section 3.2.2 makes the brackets part of the host; fiki-py's urlsplit-based authority drops them, which the conductor reports is a py defect ticked separately, so the Rust port deliberately does not match py here. THE RFC 8941 SUBSET IS STRICT where it was lenient: an integer is at most 15 digits and a decimal at most 12.3, a string carries only visible ASCII and space, a key starts with a lowercase letter or a star, leading and trailing spaces are the only ones discarded around a whole field, and a repeated dictionary or parameter key keeps its first position and takes its last value. Tokens and decimals are read rather than refused at the parser, so a value of the wrong type is reported as the field it sits in being malformed -- alg=ed25519 is a MalformedSignatureInput, sig=token a MalformedSignatureValue -- as fiki-py's parser reports it. Each choice fails closed or matches the RFC text; none was observed to change a shared or KERI vector. Rejected normalizing paths for robustness, which would let a verifier accept a request whose path differs from the one signed. Tradeoff: a caller who signs a URL one way and sends it another (a client library that re-encodes a path) now fails as SignatureMismatch, which is the profile's path-reencoded-as-hio-does case working as intended, and the IPv6 difference from fiki-py stands until py is fixed.
                The Rust port checks a field value raw, and refuses an empty method as a caller error = decision:
                  nid: 56qu7gyw
                  why: >-
                    Two defects a hostile reviewer reproduced in the js port on 2026-10-07, checked here and closed here at the KERI-parity conductor's direction. RAW VALUES: a covered field value carrying CR, LF, NUL or any other byte outside visible ASCII, space and horizontal tab is refused as SignatureMismatch on the value AS RECEIVED, and only then are leading and trailing spaces and tabs trimmed. The Rust port had trimmed first, with str::trim, which strips every Unicode whitespace character including CR and LF, so a covered field 'admin' followed by CR LF would verify as 'admin' -- a value the signer never signed reaching the application as if it had been. RFC 9110 section 5.5 makes only SP and HTAB optional whitespace around a field value, which is exactly what is trimmed now. fiki-py's str.strip has the same reach; the conductor directs ports not to copy py here. EMPTY METHOD: an empty method is InvalidArgument (@5e2phpjy) when @method is built, rather than an empty @method line, because a request has a method and an empty string is a caller who lost it; Rust cannot pass a missing one, which is where the js port rendered undefined or null. Rejected refusing an empty method when @method is not covered, since nothing then depends on it. The third js defect, a body of a byte type the API accepts being seen as empty, has no Rust counterpart: the body is one type, Option<Vec<u8>>, and its emptiness is its length. Tradeoff: a sender that pads a covered field with a stray CR now fails where it used to pass.
                A header map naming one field twice in different case is refused, and headers are canonicalized once = decision:
                  nid: 4kcthnkn
                  why: >-
                    Conductor ruling D-Q9ZT of 2026-10-07, from a hostile review of bakobo/fiki#8 at 19bb135: the Rust port takes headers as a BTreeMap<String, String>, whose keys are case-sensitive while HTTP field names are not, so a map holding both X-Role and x-role was lowered into one entry and one of the two values silently dropped -- a response signed over x-role: member verified while also carrying X-Role: admin, which an application reading the other spelling would act on. Every public function that takes a header map, the requests' and responses' alike and the Request a response answers, now refuses one holding two names equal case-insensitively, as Kind::InvalidArgument (@5e2phpjy), because assembling the map is the caller's work and a repeated HTTP field is the caller's to combine into one value per RFC 9110 section 5.3 before handing it over. Each entry point canonicalizes the map once, lowercasing names, and every later step reads that one map, so no two steps can disagree about which value a field has. Rejected picking a winner, which is the defect; rejected combining the values with a comma, which would sign a value neither copy carried. fiki-py collapses the same way through a dict comprehension, last key wins, and is not changed here. Tradeoff: a caller that copied wire headers into a map without combining repeats now gets an error where it used to get whichever value sorted last.
            fiki-go runs vectors/keri/ and carries fiki-py's KERI surface in Go's idiom = decision:
              nid: 9z57sejw
              why: >-
                fiki-go reaches parity with fiki-py on the KERI profile (@7f28p7xk, @2f227n4r, @7p9s3g9k, @6g9zjsv9): SignResponse and VerifyResponse with @status and the req parameter, a caller-chosen SignOptions.Keyid, an authoritative VerifyOptions.Resolve, minimum covered sets on both sides, ExpectedKeyid and Authorities, and section 9's order, so it runs every file under vectors/keri/ from the repository root and declares KeriVectorsFormat = 2 beside VectorsFormat. Daniel set the scope on 2026-10-07. The surface translates fiki-py's rather than transliterating it, and these are the places the two differ by more than casing. ERRORS stay one *Error whose Kind is the Python class name, as @2tt6fmc0 already chose, with five new kinds (UnknownKey, UnsupportedSigner, DuplicateComponent, InsufficientCoverage, Unauthenticated) and a Supported field beside Component for UnsupportedComponent. A CALLER'S MISTAKE, which fiki-py raises as ValueError or TypeError outside FikiError (a minimum smaller than the profile's, ExpectedAID together with Resolve, a response binding "content-digest";req verified against a Request with no Body), is returned as an error wrapping the exported sentinel ErrInvalidOptions and is never an *Error, so a caller matching on Kind cannot mistake its own bug for a bad message. Chose a returned error over a panic because Go reserves panics for states a program cannot continue from, and over a new Kind because the Kinds are a cross-language contract naming defects in messages. THE RESOLVER is func(keyid string) ([]byte, error): (nil, nil) is fiki-py's None and becomes UnknownKey, and an error the resolver returns, such as an *Error of kind UnsupportedSigner or MalformedKey, passes through unchanged, which is how fiki-py lets a resolver raise. NIL MEANS ABSENT: a nil Minimum applies no minimum, a nil Authorities checks no authority while an empty non-nil one serves nothing, a nil Request.Body means no body was handed over while an empty one is a body of no bytes, matching the nil Covered the port already reads as the default. Authorities on VerifyResponse is ErrInvalidOptions rather than ignored, because fiki-py's verify_response has no such argument and a caller who set it would otherwise believe a check ran. Rejected a separate options struct per direction, which would duplicate eight fields to exclude one. Tradeoff: the nil-versus-empty distinction is easy to miss in Go, where most APIs treat the two alike, so each one is stated on its field; and the port still holds statement rather than branch coverage, as its README already says.
              children:
                fiki-go reads the target URL and RFC 8941 as sent, beyond what the KERI vectors pin = decision:
                  nid: 8f6txftu
                  why: >-
                    Behaviour fiki-go adopts in reaching parity that no vector in vectors/ or vectors/keri/ pins, recorded because a change nothing tests is a change nobody can see. URL AS SENT: the port splits the target URL itself, as RFC 3986's generic syntax and Python's urlsplit do, rather than through net/url, because url.Parse decodes the path and @path must be the path as sent, percent-encoding included and unnormalized (profile section 2, O1); the old port read url.URL.Path and so signed a decoded path. Leading C0 controls and spaces are stripped and tab, CR and LF removed, as the WHATWG parser and urlsplit both do, a fragment is dropped, and a URL is never refused for its shape. Only @authority can fail: a port that is not a decimal from 0 to 65535 is a MissingComponent naming @authority, where fiki-py raises a bare ValueError from outside its taxonomy; failing closed with a kind is the port's choice and is not a vectors matter. IPV6: @authority keeps the brackets of an IP literal, [::1]:8443, because RFC 3986 section 3.2.2 makes them part of the host and RFC 9421 section 2.2.3 builds @authority from it; fiki-py drops them through urlsplit's hostname, which is a fiki-py bug ticked separately and is deliberately not copied, so fiki-py and fiki-go build different bases for an IPv6 target until it is fixed. The js port made the same choice on 2026-10-07. RFC 8941: the parser now reads tokens and decimals, caps an integer at 15 digits and a decimal at 12 integer and 3 fractional digits as RFC 8941 sections 3.3.1 and 3.3.2 require, overwrites a duplicated parameter in place as section 4.2.3.2 requires, and refuses a string byte outside visible ASCII as section 3.3.3 requires. Tokens and decimals are read so that a header carrying one is refused for what it means, an unsupported component parameter or a wrongly typed signature parameter, rather than as unparsable, which is the refusal fiki-py gives through http_sfv. Rejected keeping net/url and reconstructing the raw path from url.URL.RawPath, which Go only populates when its own re-encoding differs and silently normalizes otherwise. Tradeoff: a hand-rolled URL splitter is one more parser to keep right, bounded by the request vectors that carry encoded paths, a colon in a path and a re-encoded tilde.
                fiki-go refuses small-order Ed25519 keys as MalformedKey = decision:
                  nid: 8krqtpsu
                  why: >-
                    fiki-go refuses an Ed25519 public key of small order, the identity point and the other seven points whose order divides 8, in any encoding canonical or not, as MalformedKey, wherever the key comes from: a raw keyid, an ExpectedAID, or a Resolver's answer, and always before the signature is checked. This answers tick 27eo, which the conductor relayed as a requirement for every port on 2026-10-07. The reason is a forgery, not a style point: Go's crypto/ed25519, like fiki-py's OpenSSL, accepts the signature 0x01 followed by 63 zero bytes under the key 0x01 followed by 31 zero bytes over ANY message, because the verification equation [S]B = R + [k]A holds trivially when A is the identity and S is zero; the same family of shortcuts exists for every small-order A. A raw keyid is attacker-chosen, so without this check the identity point's keyid authenticates anything; a resolver is supposed to be authoritative, but a registry that can be made to hold such a key would be a forgery oracle. MalformedKey rather than SignatureMismatch, because the defect is in the key, it is found before any signature is examined, and the KERI profile's section 9 puts the key group ahead of the signature group. DETECTION without a dependency: the Edwards y coordinate is mapped to the Montgomery u = (1 + y) / (1 - y) mod 2^255 - 19 with math/big, y = 1 (the identity) caught directly, and crypto/ecdh's X25519 is used as the small-order test, since RFC 7748 scalars are clamped to multiples of 8 and Go returns an error exactly when the shared secret is all zeros, which for a clamped scalar happens exactly when the point's order divides 8; a y that names no curve point but lands on a small-order point of the quadratic twist is refused too, which is fail closed for a key that could never verify anyway. Chose that over a hard-coded blocklist of encodings, because AGENTS.md forbids byte-exact values taken from memory or a summary and a blocklist is nothing but byte-exact values, and over hand-written Edwards arithmetic, which is more code to get wrong than one field division. The tests derive the order-8 points independently, by solving d y^4 + 2 y^2 - 1 = 0 from the curve equation, so the check and its oracle do not share a method. Rejected refusing mixed-order keys (a large-order point plus a torsion component) too, which libsodium does not do and no attack here needs. Tradeoff: fiki-go now refuses a key fiki-py accepts until fiki-py is fixed, and no shared vector pins either behaviour until the next vectors_format bump adds one. EXTENDED 2026-10-07 by the conductor's ruling on tick 27eo: any 32-byte public key that is not the canonical encoding of a point on the curve is MalformedKey too, on the same paths and at the same point: a y with no x on the curve, a y at or above p even when it reduces to a valid large-order point, and the sign bit set where x is 0. Go's crypto/ed25519 already refuses such keys, but only by failing every signature, which reported them as SignatureMismatch, a forgery verdict for what is a defect in the key and one section 9 places after the key group. The check decodes y with math/big, refuses y >= p, solves x^2 = (y^2 - 1) / (d y^2 + 1) and refuses a non-square, and refuses the sign bit on x = 0; the tests classify y by Euler's criterion instead of a square root, so again the check and its oracle do not share a method.
        fiki-py decodes an RFC 8941 byte sequence the same way on every Python, with padding only at its end = decision:
          nid: 2g4xxev9
          why: >-
            fiki-py checks every byte sequence in the Signature, Signature-Input and Content-Digest headers itself, after http_sfv has parsed the header and before the value is used: its content must be base64 whose only '=' characters are the one or two that complete the final four-character quantum. Anything else -- data after the padding, an '=' in the middle, padding beyond what completes the quantum -- is refused as the header's own malformed class (MalformedSignature, MalformedSignatureInput, MalformedDigest). This answers tick 4joc, reproduced 2026-10-07 by decoding every string of up to nine characters over 'A', 'Q' and '=' on each interpreter: http_sfv decodes with Python's lenient base64.standard_b64decode, which stops at the first padding and ignores what follows through 3.12 and reads past it from 3.13, so 11,168 of those strings decode differently or fail on one side only. The split is 3.11-3.12 against 3.13-3.14, not 3.11-3.13 against 3.14 as the tick reported. In fiki's terms the same py release accepted a Signature header carrying the true signature followed by 'AAAA' on 3.11 and 3.12 and refused it on 3.13 and 3.14, and refused an '=' in the middle on 3.12 as MalformedSignatureValue while accepting it on 3.13. Grounds, read from the RFC text: RFC 8941 section 4.2.7 step 7 base64-decodes by RFC 4648, and says it does not relax RFC 4648 sections 3.1 and 3.3; section 3.3 makes the pad character before the end of the data, and excess pad characters at the end, non-alphabet data that an implementation MUST reject unless the referring specification says it may ignore them, and RFC 8941 does not say so. MISSING PADDING stays refused, on every interpreter as before. Section 4.2.7 says a parser SHOULD NOT fail when '=' padding is absent, unless it cannot be configured to accept it; http_sfv cannot, and accepting it would mean rewriting the header before handing it to the parser, a second parser in all but name. Recorded as a deviation from that SHOULD rather than fixed here. NON-ZERO PAD BITS stay accepted, as section 4.2.7 asks and as every interpreter already did. Rejected patching http_sfv's decoder, which would change it for every other user of http_sfv in the process, and rejected scanning the raw header before parsing it, which would put fiki's refusal ahead of the parser's and so change which class a doubly-malformed header gets. The scan runs on a header that has already parsed, where a colon opens a byte sequence only when it starts a bare item, never inside a string or a token. Tradeoff: excess padding ('...===') is newly refused on every interpreter, where all four accepted it; and a port that follows Python 3.14's decoder, as the C# port is reported to, still accepts an '=' in the middle that fiki-py now refuses, so the ports must converge on this rule and a vector should pin it at the next vectors_format bump.
        The C# port takes BouncyCastle for Ed25519, targets netstandard2.0 and net10.0, and ports fiki-py as it stands = decision:
          nid: 5l4p36rl
          why: >-
            The C# port lives in csharp/ and follows @2tt6fmc0's shape -- the same names in .NET casing, the error taxonomy pinned by the vectors, a required freshness choice -- with three differences, each Daniel's decision of 2026-10-07. FIRST, IT TAKES A CRYPTO DEPENDENCY, because .NET has no Ed25519 in its standard library: the API is approved (dotnet/runtime#63174) but has not shipped, and the .NET 11 release candidate adds X25519 key agreement, which is a different operation. So C# sits beside Rust in @2tt6fmc0's dependency table. Chose BouncyCastle.Cryptography over NSec.Cryptography because BouncyCastle is pure managed code: NSec wraps libsodium and ships native binaries per runtime identifier, and a native library that fails to load inside somebody's enterprise container is a failure fiki's audience (@07wstqk7: an ESB client, a cron job) would hit at the worst moment. BouncyCastle also derives the public key from a 32-byte seed directly, which spares this port the provider workaround Java needed. Rejected hand-written curve arithmetic, for the reason the Java README gives. Tradeoff: a large library of which fiki uses one algorithm, and one more maintainer outside Bakobo in the supply chain. The RFC 8941 subset stays hand-rolled, on @2q9gv70t's reasoning. SECOND, IT TARGETS netstandard2.0 AND net10.0. netstandard2.0 reaches .NET Framework 4.8, which is where many of the ESB clients @07wstqk7 names still run; net10.0 is the current LTS. Rejected net10.0 alone, which is simpler and would shut out .NET Framework entirely, and net8.0 as the floor, since its support ends in November 2026. Tradeoff: the netstandard2.0 build compiles against a smaller API, so the code is written to that floor, and CI must run the suite on .NET Framework itself (a Windows runner) for the claim to be checked rather than asserted. THIRD, THE REFERENCE IS fiki-py AS IT STANDS, NOT THE JAVA PORT. The js, go, java and rust ports still uppercase @method, which @22g0xkr8 reversed, and none of them implements the KERI profile (@8vwrexxc, @7f28p7xk, @6g9zjsv9). Porting from Java would start C# as a sixth port already behind; porting from py/src/fiki makes it the second port that passes both vectors/ and vectors/keri/. So the vector drivers assert both vectors_format and keri_vectors_format. Tradeoff: more work up front, roughly half again the size of the Java port, and the C# port will for a time be ahead of four of its siblings. Coverage is held at 100% branch through coverlet, which .NET can enforce where Java and Rust today cannot (tick 4buh).
        fiki 0.8.0 makes every port give the same answer to the same input = decision:
          nid: 5zrf8gjk
          why: >-
            The cross-port sweep of tick 6ixo (with 65q7, 6mhg and 5uvw), asked for by Daniel on 2026-10-08 and ruled by the conductor as D-GYE9. Reviews of the parity PRs #5-#10 kept finding the same input-handling holes port by port, and each port got only the findings raised against it, so the ports drifted in exactly the places no vector looked. Every rule below binds all six ports, and every rule a vector can pin is pinned at vectors_format 2 and keri_vectors_format 4, so the next drift fails a test rather than waiting for a reviewer. A caller error is the port's existing caller-mistake convention (py ValueError/TypeError, js TypeError, go ErrInvalidOptions, rust Kind::InvalidArgument, java IllegalArgumentException, C# ArgumentException), never a FikiError, and is not a vector because it is API behaviour. METHOD: wherever a request message is built, on sign and on verify, covered or not, the method must be an RFC 9110 token (1*tchar) or it is a caller error; its case is kept (@22g0xkr8). Non-empty was not enough: go signed a lone space when @method was not covered. PORTS: a port is any run of ASCII digits read as a number, so :000080 is 80, in 0..65535, and the scheme's default port is dropped. A port that is not one, in a URL handed to VERIFY, is SignatureMismatch, the profile's section 9 'base that cannot be built', never an uncaught exception (py raised a bare ValueError); on SIGN it is a caller error. SERIALIZED CALLER STRINGS: keyid, nonce and tag must be RFC 8941 sf-string content, printable ASCII 0x20-0x7E; the label must be an RFC 8941 key (lcalpha or '*', then lcalpha, DIGIT, '_', '-', '.', '*'); a plain component name must be a lowercase field name (1*tchar) or a known derived component. Anything else is a caller error at sign time, which closes CR/LF injection into Signature-Input through the one value py did not check, the label. created and expires given to a signer must be RFC 8941 integers that are non-negative, at most 15 digits, or a caller error. FRESHNESS: max_age and skew, when given, are positive integers (docs/keri-profile.md section 3); zero or negative is a caller error, declining the check (max_age None and its equivalents) still works, and arithmetic with large values saturates or compares without adding, so no port wraps. VERDICT: keyid is the keyid exactly as it arrived, or null when there was none; aid is the identity that vouched for the key -- the non-transferable AID of a raw key, or the keyid a resolver or expected_aid vouched for -- and each port's doc comment says so. Rejected 'the AID the resolver vouched for' as keyid's meaning, because that is what aid already carries and two fields saying one thing leave the wire value unreported. EXPORTS: every port exports VECTORS_FORMAT and KERI_VECTORS_FORMAT in its own spelling. BOUNDS (65q7, 6mhg), size before shape: Signature-Input, Signature and Content-Digest are each at most 8192 bytes, checked before the field is parsed; a dictionary holds at most 16 members, an inner list at most 64 items, an item at most 16 parameters. Over a limit is that header's malformed class (MalformedSignatureInput, MalformedSignature, MalformedDigest), never a crash or a slow parse. The limits are module constants, exported where a port exports constants, and named in the user guide. They are far above anything an honest signer sends -- the profile's own messages carry one label, at most a dozen components and six parameters -- and low enough that no parser's cost matters. Rejected leaving bounds to each HTTP server's header limit, because fiki is handed header values by callers that may have applied none. 5uvw: a padding-bit alias of a B keyid is malformed-key, and a response covering "content-digest";req whose request body contradicts the request's Content-Digest is digest-mismatch, both now vectors. Tradeoff: every port changes at once and every port's vectors-format assertion fails until it does; the bounds refuse some message RFC 9421 permits, deliberately.
          children:
            fiki-py's reading of the 0.8.0 rulings, where the spec leaves a choice = decision:
              nid: 9g24rdns
              why: >-
                Choices fiki-py makes as the reference implementation of @5zrf8gjk that the sweep spec does not dictate, recorded so the five other ports can match them or say why not. URL READ LAZILY: the target URL is split when a component that needs it is built, so an unreadable authority (a bad port, text after an IP-literal's ']', an unbalanced bracket) surfaces where section 9 puts a base that cannot be built, after the key and coverage checks, and a request that does not cover @authority is not refused for a port it never signed. fiki-py parses the authority itself rather than trusting urlsplit's port and hostname, which vary across Python releases and raise ValueError outside fiki's taxonomy. A PORT has its leading zeros stripped first, and more than five digits left over is out of range, so no digit string of unbounded length is ever converted to a number; Python refuses one of over 4300 digits with a ValueError of its own. An IP-LITERAL is unreadable unless the text inside its brackets is an IPv6 address, with an optional zone after '%', or IPvFuture ('v', hex digits, '.', then at least one character). urlsplit checks this only from Python 3.11.4, and Debian 12 ships 3.11.2, so fiki-py checks it itself, and every other port checks the same grammar. A SIGNATURE HEADER whose value cannot be encoded as UTF-8, such as one holding an unpaired surrogate, is that header's malformed class, like any other value that cannot be read. An EMPTY port ('host:') stays what RFC 3986 section 6.2.3 makes it, no port at all, as py has always read it. A Host header on a relative URL is still taken verbatim and lowercased, with no port parsing, since without a scheme there is no default port to drop. A SUPPLIED CONTENT-DIGEST that does not parse, names no algorithm fiki computes, or does not match the body is a caller error (ValueError) when signing with a body, in sign_request and in sign_response's own digest; the request digest sign_response binds keeps raising DigestMismatch or MalformedDigest, as bakobo/fiki#4 decided. HEADERS: a name or value that is not a str, None included, is a TypeError when the mapping is canonicalized. COMPONENT NAMES: a caller's plain name is still lowercased as a convenience and then must be 1*tchar, so X-Role is accepted as x-role and 'x-a CR LF' is refused; a name beginning with '@' keeps its UnsupportedComponent refusal, which names the component as the profile requires. TYPES: created, expires, max_age and skew must be int and not bool, a TypeError otherwise, because bool is an int in Python and True would serialize as a bare RFC 8941 boolean. STRICT SFV: http_sfv already refuses integers over fifteen digits; fiki adds its own scan for a decimal with no fractional digit ('1.'), beside the byte-sequence scan of @2g4xxev9, run on a header that has already parsed. BOUNDS: the item limits apply to every member, inner list and item of all three headers, counted on the parsed structure, and are exported as MAX_FIELD_BYTES, MAX_DICTIONARY_MEMBERS, MAX_INNER_LIST_ITEMS and MAX_PARAMETERS. VECTORS: the Content-Length rule is pinned in vectors/keri/ rather than the shared set, because the shared driver verifies with no minimum and so applies no body rule; shared accept cases now carry the verdict's keyid. Tradeoff: fiki-py owns a small authority parser, and its edge cases are its own to get right.
            fiki-js's reading of the 0.8.0 rulings, where the spec leaves a choice = decision:
              nid: 0e832nug
              why: >-
                Choices fiki-js makes in meeting @5zrf8gjk that the sweep spec does not dictate, beside fiki-py's @9g24rdns. CALLER ERRORS are all TypeError: JavaScript has no ValueError, so a method that is not a token, a bad label, keyid, nonce, tag or alg, a component name that is not a field name, created or expires outside 0..999999999999999, a non-positive or non-integer maxAge or skew, a header value that is not a string, a supplied Content-Digest the body does not bear out, and an unreadable URL when signing are each a TypeError and never a FikiError. TYPES: Number.isInteger decides an integer, so a BigInt, a string, a boolean, NaN, Infinity and 1.5 are refused alike; a JavaScript number cannot overflow, and with created and expires capped at fifteen digits the freshness arithmetic stays exact, so no saturation is needed. HEADER NAMES cannot be null or non-strings in a plain object, whose keys are always strings, so A4's check falls on values only; Symbol-keyed entries are not enumerated and so are not headers. BOUNDS: the byte bound is measured on the raw value in UTF-8 before parsing; the counts are enforced inside the hand-rolled RFC 8941 parser as each member, item and parameter is added, so an over-long list is refused without being read to its end, and the four MAX_* constants live in sfv.js and are exported from the package. Duplicate dictionary keys and parameters count once, as the parsed structure holds them, matching fiki-py. STRICT SFV: the parser never supported decimals or tokens, so '1.' was already refused; the byte-sequence check moved from a loose regex plus a lenient atob to RFC 4648 canonical padding. URL: the authority was already split by fiki's own code rather than new URL (@90y0gsfx); it now also refuses a bracket outside an IP-literal, and an IP-literal that is not an IPv6 address or IPvFuture, as urlsplit does, and carries a received flag so the same unreadable port is a TypeError when signing and SignatureMismatch when verifying. Read lazily, since the regex split never fails and only the authority step can. URL CLEANING, at the conductor's ruling of 2026-10-08 that every port build fiki-py's base: before splitting, fiki-js strips leading C0 controls and spaces and removes TAB, CR and LF anywhere, as urlsplit does. Like urlsplit and unlike WHATWG it does not strip the trailing end, so a trailing space is signed and a trailing control in a covered component is SignatureMismatch. Tradeoff: fiki-js's caller errors are one class where fiki-py splits them in two, so a caller cannot tell a wrong type from a wrong value by class alone; the message says which.
            fiki-go's reading of the 0.8.0 rulings, where the spec leaves a choice = decision:
              nid: 56ur23u6
              why: >-
                Choices the Go port makes implementing @5zrf8gjk that neither the spec nor @9g24rdns dictates, or where Go's idiom differs from fiki-py's. NO NULLS: a Go string cannot be nil, so A4's null names and values have no Go spelling, and Verdict.Keyid is the empty string, not null, when the signature carried no keyid; likewise a zero Created means 'now' and a zero Expires means absent, as they always have, so a signer cannot put created=0 on the wire. A9 holds by the language: Go maps have no inherited members, and a test pins names such as __proto__ and constructor as ordinary. BLANK FIELDS: a Signature or Signature-Input holding only SP and HTAB counts as absent, MissingSignature or MissingSignatureInput, and a 401 with such a Signature is Unauthenticated; fiki-py reads such a value as present and fails it at parsing. Kept, because RFC 9110 section 5.5 makes the surrounding whitespace no part of the value, so an all-whitespace field is an empty one, and go already tested it. BOUNDS are enforced inside the hand-rolled parser as it reads, counting distinct members and parameters, rather than on a parsed result; the refusal is the header's malformed kind either way. FRESHNESS: Skew nil is DefaultSkew and there is no way to decline it, as in py; the comparisons add only non-negative quantities, through a saturating helper, and never subtract, so no Now, MaxAge or Skew can wrap. AUTHORITY: every reason the authority cannot be read -- a port that is not 0..65535, text after an IP-literal, an IP-literal that is not an IPv6 address or IPvFuture, an unclosed or stray bracket -- is one refusal, SignatureMismatch received and ErrInvalidOptions signing; a relative URL with no Host header stays MissingComponent both ways. URL WHITESPACE (spec section E, open): splitURL strips leading C0 controls and spaces and removes TAB, CR and LF anywhere, as urlsplit and WHATWG do, so Go and py agree. Tradeoff: the blank-field reading diverges from py on a message no vector pins.
            fiki-rs's reading of the 0.8.0 rulings, where the spec leaves a choice = decision:
              nid: 2n99rej7
              why: >-
                Choices the Rust port makes in implementing @5zrf8gjk that neither the sweep spec nor fiki-py's reading (@9g24rdns) dictates, recorded so a reviewer can tell idiom from drift. TYPES: fiki-py's TypeError rules (a header name or value, a method, a label, created, expires, max_age or skew of the wrong type, None included) have no Rust counterpart, because &str, String and i64 make those inputs unrepresentable; there is nothing to refuse and no test to write. A NEGATIVE created or expires, which i64 can hold, is InvalidArgument like an over-long one. SKEW: VerifyOptions.skew is Option<i64>, and None takes DEFAULT_SKEW rather than declining anything, as it always has here; py refuses skew=None because py has no other spelling of 'the default'. Some(0) and below are InvalidArgument, and max_age: None still declines the age check. NO WRAPPING: freshness compares in i128, where no sum or difference of two i64 can overflow, rather than saturating; so a clock at i64::MIN with a skew of i64::MAX is answered (SignatureTooOld, which is correct) instead of panicking in a debug build or wrapping in a release one. PORTS: leading zeros are stripped before the digits are parsed, so a run of any length reads as its number without overflowing a u32, and an all-zero port is 0. The authority is split by hand, as split_target always was: userinfo dropped at the last '@', an IP-literal kept with its brackets and allowed only a ':port' after its ']', and a ']' or '[' anywhere else unreadable; the text inside the brackets must be an IPv6 address, with an optional zone, or IPvFuture. That matches what urlsplit refuses for py. MESSAGE: the received-or-signing distinction rides on the internal Message, so the same parser answers SignatureMismatch on verify and InvalidArgument on sign. BOUNDS: counted after parsing, as py does, which is safe because the 8192-byte check has already run; parameters are counted on every item and on every inner list. A Content-Digest that is absent when read is MalformedDigest, the kind it always was here. URL WHITESPACE (the spec's open item, ruled by the conductor on 2026-10-08 to match py, go and js): the URL is read as Python's urlsplit reads it, leading C0 controls and spaces stripped, trailing ones kept, and TAB, CR and LF removed wherever they are. Before that ruling Rust kept all three, so CR and LF reached value_of as SignatureMismatch and a TAB was signed as given. Tradeoff: a URL with a line break in it is silently cleaned rather than refused, which is WHATWG's choice and now every port's.
            fiki-java's reading of the 0.8.0 rulings, where the spec leaves a choice = decision:
              nid: 420qvvxw
              why: >-
                Choices fiki-java makes in meeting @5zrf8gjk that the sweep spec does not dictate, beside fiki-py's in @9g24rdns. CALLER ERRORS are all IllegalArgumentException, Java having no TypeError: a null header name or value, a null method, and every case py splits between TypeError and ValueError. URL: fiki-java keeps its own lexical split (Target.split), which before 0.8.0 stripped nothing, so a TAB inside a URL reached the signature base as a TAB and a CR or LF in a covered @path, @query or @authority was SignatureMismatch even on the signing side. The spec left this open, and the conductor then ruled on 2026-10-08 that every port match py and go: TAB, CR and LF anywhere in the URL are removed before it is read, as Python's urlsplit and the WHATWG URL standard remove them. Only those three; other C0 controls still reach the base-value check and are refused there. The port is read lazily, only when @authority is built, so E1 holds without a separate path; an empty port is no port, and leading zeros are stripped before the length check so no padding reaches parseInt. SUPPLIED CONTENT-DIGEST: a FikiException from parsing or comparing the caller's own digest at signing is rethrown as IllegalArgumentException carrying it as the cause; the request digest signResponse binds keeps raising DigestMismatch or MalformedDigest, as bakobo/fiki#4 decided and py keeps. CONTENT-LENGTH: String.strip is replaced by an SP/HTAB trim shared with field values, since strip also takes VT, FF, CR and the Unicode spaces. STRICT SFV: the byte-sequence padding rule is enforced inside Sfv's parser as a length that is a multiple of four, and Java's basic decoder already refuses '=' before the end and any character outside the alphabet, CR and LF included, so no post-parse scan like py's @2g4xxev9 is needed. BOUNDS: the field size is UTF-8 bytes of the raw value, before parsing; the counts are taken on the parsed structure, after RFC 8941's last-wins deduplication of repeated keys, as py's http_sfv counts them, and the parameter bound applies to each dictionary member, each inner list and each item inside it. The four limits are public static final ints on Fiki. A9: Java's maps have no inherited members, so the analogue is a test that names like constructor and hashcode are refused as signature parameters and ignored as digest algorithms. B16: created and expires are refused below 0 as well as above fifteen digits in signatureBase and responseSignatureBase too, so a test needing a negative created now signs the base directly as a foreign signer would. Tradeoff: fiki-java still owns its URL splitter, whose edge cases beyond these are its own to get right. AFTER THE HOSTILE REVIEW OF bakobo/fiki#14, the authority is checked before it is lowercased, a bracket outside an IP-literal is refused (https://a]b/x), and an IP-literal whose contents are neither an IPv6 address (with an optional non-empty zone holding no further %) nor IPvFuture is refused: SignatureMismatch on verify, IllegalArgumentException on sign, as in every other port.
            fiki-csharp's reading of the 0.8.0 rulings, where the spec leaves a choice = decision:
              nid: 7mwnjlws
              why: >-
                Choices the C# port makes implementing @5zrf8gjk that neither the spec nor @9g24rdns dictates, or where .NET's idiom differs from fiki-py's. NULLS: a null header name or value is an ArgumentException where the headers are snapshotted once (HeaderSnapshot.Take), on every signing and verifying entry point and for the paired Request, beside the duplicate-name refusal of D-Q9ZT. FRESHNESS WINDOWS are refused where they are stated, by VerifyOptions.MaxAge and WithSkew, as ArgumentOutOfRangeException (an ArgumentException), rather than when verify runs, because VerifyOptions is an immutable value and an invalid one should never exist; DecliningFreshness still declines the age check, and the comparisons stay in decimal, so no long can wrap. CALLER STRINGS are checked at the public entry before anything about the message: the label, then created, expires, keyid, alg, nonce and tag, then the method, so a CR or LF is refused by name rather than surfacing as a later FikiException; SignatureBase and ResponseSignatureBase check alg too. URL: the message keeps the URL as a string and splits it on first use. This port's urlsplit reproduction (PyUrl) reads the whole URL, so its refusals -- an unbalanced bracket, text after an IP-literal's ']', a bracketed host that is not IPv6 or IPvFuture, the NFKC check -- fire for @path and @query as well as @authority, which E1 allows; a port that is not 0..65535 fires only for @authority. Every one is SignatureMismatch on a received message and ArgumentException when signing. The host is lowercased whole, an IPv6 zone included, as fiki-py 0.8.0 does. SUPPLIED DIGEST: the signer reuses the verifier's own digest reading and comparison, so a supplied Content-Digest over the 8192-byte bound, unparsable, naming nothing fiki computes or contradicted by the body is one ArgumentException carrying the verifier's reason. BOUNDS: the byte count is the UTF-8 length of the value as received, before any trimming; members, inner-list items and parameters are counted on the parsed dictionary, as py counts them, and apply to every inner list and item of all three headers. The parser itself stays unbounded for a caller's own covered list, which A6's linear work covers. URL WHITESPACE (spec section E, open): PyUrl strips leading C0 controls and spaces and removes TAB, CR and LF anywhere, as urlsplit does, so C#, py and go agree. Tradeoff: a window refused at construction is a different call site from py's, though the same class of error.
    The key travels inline in keyid, as raw bytes rather than as the AID = decision:
      nid: 7xrx5evg
      why: >-
        The signer's key travels in RFC 9421's own keyid parameter, as the RAW 32-byte Ed25519 public key in base64url with no padding -- the RFC 8037 JWK "x" form, 43 characters -- and NOT as the 44-character B-prefix AID. So a request carries its own verifying key inline and a verifier resolves nothing; the AID remains what a human registers and reads, and fiki derives it from the keyid on the verify side rather than transmitting it. This is not a fresh choice: heti's vanilla dialect already fixes it at src/heti/ephemeral/vanilla.py:_resolve_key, whose @c3m5wp chose the inline raw-key form over a directory lookup because both of its recovery paths must stay pure, and cited draft-hardt-httpbis-signature-key and Cloudflare's web-bot-auth as the external convention. fiki emitting the AID there instead would mean heti could not verify a fiki-signed request at all, which would defeat the interop the split exists to preserve, so the decision is inherited rather than reopened. Chose one form rather than accepting both, because a keyid a verifier has to sniff is an interop ambiguity that shows up as a signature failure at somebody else's site. Chose to keep sign_request's keyid NON-overridable, unlike the lower-level signature_base, so that "the keyid is the key" is a property of every fiki-signed request rather than a default: the RFC's own Appendix B.2.6 vector uses a symbolic keyid ("test-key-ed25519") and is reproduced through signature_base for exactly that reason. Rejected carrying the AID in a separate header the way the KERI dialect carries Signify-Resource, which would put the identity outside RFC 9421's own vocabulary and give an off-the-shelf verifier one more thing it does not implement. Tradeoff: a verifier holding a preregistered AID must convert it to raw bytes to compare against keyid, which is two lines but is a conversion that would not exist if the AID travelled directly; and a fiki-signed request is pseudonymous by construction, since an inline key attests to itself and to nothing else.
      children:
        A caller may choose the keyid, and a caller-supplied resolver is authoritative for it = decision:
          nid: 6g9zjsv9
          why: >-
            sign_request and the new sign_response accept a caller-supplied keyid string, such as a KERI AID, and verify_request and verify_response accept a caller-supplied resolver, a callable from the keyid string to the 32 raw bytes of an Ed25519 public key. Without either, nothing changes: the keyid is still the inline raw key of @7xrx5evg and a verifier still decodes it. This revises one clause of @7xrx5evg, that sign_request's keyid is not overridable. The KERI profile makes keyid the signer's AID (profile R1), a transferable AID does not contain its current key, and a signify-ts or keripy verifier has to resolve it against a key event log, so fiki cannot produce the profile's vectors while "the keyid is the key" is a property of every fiki-signed request. Daniel decided on 2026-09-24 to keep fiki KERI-free and hand the resolution to the caller: fiki learns nothing about CESR beyond the non-transferable B-code lens it already has, and a caller who holds KEL state supplies the function that reads it. WHEN A RESOLVER IS SUPPLIED IT IS AUTHORITATIVE and fiki never falls back to decoding the keyid as a key. That is the safety property, not a detail: a basic transferable prefix D... embeds its inception key, and a verifier that decoded it would accept a signature from a key rotated away, undoing pre-rotation (profile R1, [SPC-F6]). A resolver that returns None means the keyid names no key the caller knows, which is UnknownKey, a new class; a resolver may itself raise MalformedKey for a keyid that is not a well-formed identifier, and fiki propagates it. In resolver mode the Verdict's aid is the keyid the resolver vouched for, since the key's own B rendering is not the signer's identity there. Supplying both expected_aid and a resolver is a programming error, refused with TypeError, because the two answer the same question and a silent precedence rule would be a trap. Rejected teaching fiki to decode transferable prefixes itself, which is the start of a CESR implementation and the start of keripy's dependency surface (@07wstqk7). Rejected making the keyid form a verifier sniffs from its first character, which the profile forbids outright because D... would sniff as a key. Tradeoff: a fiki-signed request with a caller-chosen keyid is no longer self-verifying, so the pseudonymous inline-key property of @7xrx5evg now holds only for the default; and resolver correctness is entirely the caller's, including rotation.
        fiki-py refuses a public key that is small-order or not a canonical on-curve encoding, as MalformedKey = decision:
          nid: 37wdchu5
          why: >-
            fiki-py refuses, as MalformedKey and before any signature is examined, an Ed25519 public key that is not the canonical encoding of a point on the curve -- y at or above p, a y with no x on the curve, or the sign bit set where x is 0 -- or that is a point of small order, the identity and the seven other points whose order divides 8. That holds wherever the key comes from: a raw keyid, an AID decoded by verifying_key (so expected_aid too), or a resolver's answer. This is tick 27eo and the conductor's decisions of 2026-10-07 (D-C3BH, widened by D-QMDN), which every port follows. The reason is a forgery: fiki-py, through cryptography and OpenSSL, accepted the signature 0x01 followed by 63 zero bytes under the key 0x01 followed by 31 zero bytes over ANY message, because [S]B = R + [k]A holds trivially when A is the identity and S is zero; a raw keyid is attacker-chosen, so that keyid authenticated anything, and a registry that could be made to hold such a key would make a resolver a forgery oracle. An off-curve key could never verify, but fiki-py reported it as SignatureMismatch, a forgery verdict for a defect in the key, which the KERI profile's section 9 orders after the key group. MalformedKey because the defect is the key's and is found first. DETECTION IN PURE PYTHON, with no new dependency (@07wstqk7 fixes fiki-py's dependencies at two): the key is decompressed exactly as RFC 8032 section 5.1.3 decodes a point, refusing what that procedure refuses, and the point is then doubled three times in extended coordinates (RFC 8032 section 5.1.4's doubling) and refused if the result is the identity, which is the definition of an order dividing 8. cryptography exposes neither test. Rejected a hard-coded blocklist of encodings in the libsodium style, because AGENTS.md forbids byte-exact values taken from memory and a blocklist is nothing but such values; the tests instead derive the eight small-order points from the curve equation (order-8 points satisfy d y^4 + 2 y^2 - 1 = 0) with a general square root, so the check and its oracle do not share a method, and cover all fourteen encodings libsodium's has_small_order names. Rejected refusing mixed-order keys, which libsodium does not do and no attack here needs. Tradeoff: about forty lines of field arithmetic in keys.py, run once per verification, and a key fiki-py used to accept now fails; no honest key can be small-order or off-curve, since a clamped secret scalar times the base point is neither. No shared vector pins this until the next vectors_format bump.
        'fiki takes an Ed25519 key as an AID, raw base64url, did:key, did:peer:0, or an OpenSSH key, and nothing changes on the wire = decision':
          nid: 0mvgkwnl
          why: >-
            fiki accepts an Ed25519 public key in five spellings and an Ed25519 signing key in one more, all at the edge of the API and none on the wire. A new function, aid_from in each port's idiom, takes text that names a public key and returns the canonical AID: the AID itself; the raw key as 43 characters of unpadded base64url, which is the wire keyid of @7xrx5evg and also the "x" member of an RFC 8037 JWK; a did:key whose multibase value is base58btc ("z") over the multicodec ed25519-pub header 0xed 0x01 and 32 bytes -- the did:key ABNF also admits base64url ("u"), but its resolution algorithm requires "z" or raises invalidDid and the peer DID ABNF has transform = "z", and reading both would give every key two DIDs, so "u" is refused (revised after the hostile review of 2026-10-08, which caught the first draft accepting it); a did:peer with numalgo 0, which the peer DID spec defines as equivalent to the did:key with the same multibase value; and an OpenSSH public-key line, "ssh-ed25519", one space, the base64 blob, and optionally one space and a printable-ASCII comment that neither begins nor ends with a space. aid_from never quotes its input in an error and leaves keyid empty, because what a caller hands it by mistake can be a private key -- the .key file for the .pub, or a seed, which in base64url is 43 characters like a raw key. A new constructor, Key.from_openssh in each port's idiom, loads an UNENCRYPTED openssh-key-v1 private key, so a party that already has ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 can sign with it. The driving constraint is reach: people who will never hold a CESR string already hold one of these, and the conversion is lossless because every one of them carries the same 32 bytes. Every spelling is parsed strictly and has exactly one accepted form, refused as MalformedKey otherwise: a bounded length before anything is decoded (1024 characters for a public key, 4096 for a private one), canonical base64 and base64url only, base58btc that re-encodes to the same text so a leading "1" cannot add a zero byte, the multicodec header as exactly those two bytes so a non-minimal varint cannot name the same codec twice, no blob trailing bytes, and an OpenSSH private key whose two check integers agree, whose padding is the 1, 2, 3 sequence, whose embedded public key matches both the public section and the seed, and which holds exactly one key. Every decoded key then goes through the same small-order and canonical-point refusal as a keyid (@37wdchu5), so no spelling is a way around it. Chose a converter called at registration time over making expected_aid accept every spelling, because expected_aid sits inside the security comparison and @7xrx5evg already rejected a value a verifier has to sniff; a converter fails at configuration load, where a mistake is cheap, rather than on a live request at somebody else's site. Chose to accept only the JWK's "x" member rather than a JWK document, because Rust's standard library has no JSON parser and netstandard2.0 has no System.Text.Json, so a JWK document would add a dependency to two ports, and "x" is already the 43-character form. Chose unencrypted private keys only and rejected encrypted ones, because decrypting openssh-key-v1 needs bcrypt-pbkdf and AES-CTR, which are dependencies in most ports, including golang.org/x/crypto in Go; the refusal tells the caller to keep a dedicated unencrypted key for fiki rather than strip the passphrase from one used elsewhere. Rejected ssh-agent signing for now, since it is a socket protocol per platform and a larger surface than everything above combined. Signatures do not cross protocols: SSH user authentication (RFC 4252 section 7) signs data beginning with a length-prefixed session identifier, so its first byte is 0x00, and SSHSIG signs data beginning "SSHSIG", while every base sign_request and sign_response build begins with a double quote. That is not enough to make a login key safe to register, and the first draft of this node said it was: an ssh-agent signs whatever bytes it is asked to, so with agent forwarding root on any forwarded-to host can sign fiki requests with a login key. The guide therefore says to use a dedicated key that is never loaded into an agent. Rejected other key types: secp256k1 and Ed448 have no algorithm in RFC 9421's registry, so supporting them would end fiki's claim to be standard RFC 9421; P-256 does have one (ecdsa-p256-sha256) and a real constituency in HSMs, cloud KMS, Secure Enclave and FIPS shops, but it means binding alg to the key, a second keyid shape that @7xrx5evg's no-sniffing rule forbids, and ECDSA signatures that are not deterministic unless RFC 6979 is used, so it is deferred until a named consumer needs it rather than built speculatively. The cases live in a vector set of their own, vectors/keys/, with its own monotonic key_vectors_format starting at 1, because the README holds that adding a case to an existing set is breaking, and a separate number lets each port ship the converter on its own schedule without moving vectors_format 2. Its inputs are corroborated outside fiki where they can be: the did:key spec's own example DIDs, the peer DID spec's stated equivalence, base58btc from an independent package at generation time, and OpenSSH keys written by ssh-keygen itself. Tradeoff: six ports each carry a base58 decoder and an openssh-key-v1 reader that fiki did not need before, roughly 100 to 150 lines a port, and the error surface for a bad registration is MalformedKey rather than a new class, so heti's exception mapping is unchanged.
    'fiki reads a clock: expires is always enforced, max_age is a required decision = decision':
      nid: 67shl6c5
      why: >-
        fiki reads a clock, and verify_request enforces freshness. Two conditions, decided separately because they have different owners. EXPIRES IS ALWAYS ENFORCED and takes no parameter: the signer put it there, it is bound into the signature base so it cannot be tampered with, and RFC 9421 section 3.2.1 says a verifier may reject a signature ahead of its expiration but never that it may ignore one -- a library that accepts an expires it does not honor is selling the caller a guarantee it has not bought, which is the defect ~3qdj named. MAX AGE IS A REQUIRED KEYWORD with no default, so a caller states a tolerance in seconds or states None, and either way the decision is written at the call site. Chose a required argument over a default because both defaults are wrong: a value guesses at somebody else's clock skew and replay window, and None reproduces the silent skip this whole change exists to remove -- the same fail-closed reasoning @2hwvpm42 used for the body. Section 3.2.1 is the authority for both halves: it lists maximum-age enforcement and expiry rejection as APPLICATION requirements rather than baseline ones, and then says that when an application defines a requirement it MUST enforce it during verification. So the RFC does not tell fiki what the policy is; it tells fiki that a declared policy must bite. A skew allowance defaults to 5 seconds and is adjustable, because two hosts disagreeing by a second is ordinary and a verifier that treats that as an attack is unusable. `now` is injectable, which is what keeps @5gf6r08f's vectors byte-exact: a conformance vector cannot pin a check against a moving clock, and a port would otherwise have to fake time to run them. fiki reads the clock ONLY when it needs to -- when expires is present, or when max_age is not None -- so a request that declares no freshness at all still verifies deterministically. Rejected enforcing a default max_age: it would break every existing vector, all of which are signed at a fixed created in the past, and breaking them is the loud symptom of a guess nobody made deliberately. Rejected leaving expires to the same required-parameter treatment as max_age: the two are not symmetric, since expires is the SIGNER's declaration and max_age is the VERIFIER's, and requiring a verifier to opt in to honoring a signer's own expiry inverts who is asserting what. Tradeoff: verify_request is no longer a pure function, so fiki's purity claim now has an exception with a name; heti's vanilla dialect must pass max_age through and passes None explicitly, which is heti's standing position under @p3w5kq rather than an oversight, and a heti caller who wants freshness now has somewhere for that to go.
      children:
        In Go and Rust a forgotten max_age is a caller error, not the decline, and Go's optional strings are pointers = decision:
          nid: 65u2932c
          why: >-
            From the pre-0.9.0 review panel of 2026-10-09 (finding API-F1). @67shl6c5 makes max_age a required decision because skipping the check silently is the thing the field exists to prevent, and Python, JavaScript, Java (Freshness.DECLINED, @3e7wnyvg) and C# (DecliningFreshness) enforce it. Go and Rust did not: Go's MaxAge *int64 and Rust's max_age Option<i64> read a field the caller never wrote, the zero value or ..Default::default(), as the explicit decline, so a verifier that forgot the decision accepted replays forever with no error. Go now pairs MaxAge with AnyAge bool, as it pairs Authorities with AnyAuthority and ExpectedKeyid with AnyKeyid: exactly one is stated, and neither or both is ErrInvalidOptions. Rust's max_age becomes a MaxAge enum, Unstated by default, MaxAge::Seconds(n) or MaxAge::Unchecked, as Authorities and ExpectedKeyid already are; Unstated is Kind::InvalidArgument when verify_request or verify_response runs, since ..Default::default() cannot make a field mandatory. In the same release, and for the same reason, tick 5kyt: Go's ExpectedAID and request-side ExpectedKeyid become *string, so an empty string from a missing configuration value is a stated empty AID, refused as the caller error the other ports raise, and no longer reads as unset; Daniel chose *string on 2026-10-09 because Skew is already *int64. Both land in 0.9.0 rather than a later release because 0.9.0 already breaks every Go and Rust verifier, and one break is cheaper for a caller than two. No vector changes: the drivers already map a vector's null max_age to the explicit decline, and the misuse vector for an empty expected_aid waits for vectors format 4. Rejected a positive-only int64 with zero as unset in Go, which keeps the silent default for anyone who writes nothing. Tradeoff: every Go and Rust verifier call site changes in 0.9.0, including ones that already passed a max_age, in Go only if they relied on nil.
    '@method is the method as sent, with no case transformation = decision':
      nid: 22g0xkr8
      why: >-
        fiki stops uppercasing @method. The component value is the method exactly as the caller supplies it, because RFC 9421 section 2.2.1 defines @method as the method as sent, case-sensitive, with no transformation, and HTTP methods are case-sensitive tokens (RFC 9110 section 9.1): "post" and "POST" are different methods, and a verifier that folds them computes the same base for both. Daniel's decision of 2026-09-24, prompted by the KERI profile (@8vwrexxc), which pins a lowercase-method vector so every implementation behaves the same. The consequence the RFC anticipates is the caller's burden: browsers' fetch() uppercases the standard methods on the wire, so a signer has to be handed the method as it will actually be sent. Chose to fix fiki-py only in this change and file ticks for js, go, java and rust, all four of which uppercase today, because the shared vectors exercise only uppercase methods and are unaffected, so no port goes red and vectors_format does not move -- the fix is observable only with a lowercase method, which none of the shared vectors carries. That is also why it is not a format bump under @4fhrre0m: no shared vector's required behaviour changed. heti and heti-wallet consume fiki; they are affected only when a caller signs or verifies a lowercase method, which after this change must be sent in the case it was signed in. Rejected keeping the uppercase fold as a convenience: it is a normalization the RFC does not permit, and it makes a fiki verifier accept a request whose method differs from the one signed. Tradeoff: until the four ticks land, fiki-py and the other ports disagree on lowercase methods while all five declare vectors_format 1, which is exactly the drift @4fhrre0m's number exists to expose and here cannot, because no shared vector pins it.
    fiki's vectors_format 3 makes the verifier fail closed by default and closes the 2026-10-08 hostile review = decision:
      nid: 524c8qgv
      why: >-
        vectors_format 3 is the sweep that follows the input-hardening review of 2026-10-08 (five fresh-context reviewers). No reviewer forged a signature in any port. They found that a verifier left at its defaults accepts what fiki's own signer never produces -- a body nothing covers, a covered list of nothing, a signature with no created that replays forever -- so the README's headline protections held on the signing side only; that five of six ports let a request target choose @authority; that fiki-js parses RFC 8941 strings leniently; and that the Ed25519 check itself was never chosen and differs in C#. Every rule below binds all six ports and is pinned by vectors at vectors_format 3, which is breaking by construction: verifier defaults change, so a port at format 2 would accept requests a format-3 port refuses. Chose to bundle these into one format bump rather than one per finding, because each bump is six coordinated releases and a consumer upgrade, and the review's findings are one defect class (fail-open defaults and lenient parsing at the verifier). Split across more than one pull request where the diff demands it, with no release between them, so format 3 is never shipped half-built. Tradeoff: heti, which verifies through fiki's defaults in src/heti/ephemeral/vanilla.py, must make two decisions explicitly when it next bumps fiki (see the children), and a verifier upgraded from 0.8 starts refusing requests from any signer that covers less than fiki's own default.
      children:
        verify_request applies fiki's own signing default as a minimum covered set unless the caller opts out = decision:
          nid: 9hmrfqqj
          why: >-
            verify_request applies a minimum covered set by default: @method, @authority, @path and @query, plus content-digest whenever the request has a body by the profile's test (a non-empty body, any Transfer-Encoding, or a Content-Length above zero, so a body the caller forgot to pass still counts when the headers announce it). Being a minimum, it also brings what every minimum brings: created is required (@7p9s3g9k) and keyid is required even beside expected_aid (@7y9lfnzq). minimum=None is the explicit opt-out, and a supplied minimum still may not drop below the KERI profile's REQUEST_MINIMUM. The constant is DEFAULT_MINIMUM. Chose a default over the status quo because the status quo made fiki's headline claims true only for a verifier that read verdict.covered, which no sample in the guide did: a signature that omits @query cannot tell ?limit=1 from ?limit=1000000 (@2hwvpm42), and the 0.8 default accepted exactly that signature; it also accepted a body nothing covered, an uncovered Content-Digest contradicting the body, the empty list sig=() that binds nothing but its own parameters and so authenticates any request within its freshness window, and a signature with no created that replays forever (review V-S1, V-S7, B2). Chose fiki's own signing default, @authority included, as the floor rather than the profile's REQUEST_MINIMUM, which omits @authority: covering it does not stop an attacker who also forges Host, but it makes a cross-service replay depend on the victim's routing accepting a foreign Host, and it costs fiki's own clients nothing because DEFAULT_COVERED already covers it; Daniel ruled this on 2026-10-08 (D-B796) after debate, reversing the assistant's first position that @authority without a list of served hosts checks nothing. Rejected requiring only the body rule, because the same argument applies to @query, which is the README's headline. Tradeoff: a non-fiki RFC 9421 signer that covers less (a KERI-profile signer omitting @authority, for instance) is refused unless the verifier passes its own minimum or None, and heti's vanilla dialect must choose one when it bumps fiki. verify_response keeps its existing default for now; that asymmetry is ticked rather than decided here.
        authorities is a required decision, a collection of hosts or None, never a string = decision:
          nid: 3nd9yugl
          why: >-
            authorities becomes a required argument of verify_request, as max_age is (@67shl6c5): the caller passes the collection of @authority values it serves, or None, and either way the decision is written at the call site. A collection is a set, list, tuple or other non-string collection of strings; a bare string, bytes, or a collection holding a non-string is a caller error in the port's idiom (@5zrf8gjk's convention), never a FikiError, because fiki-py turned authorities="api.example.com" into a substring test that admitted example.com (review A3) and fiki-js failed closed on the same input. Chose a required decision over a default because both defaults are wrong for the reason @67shl6c5 gave for max_age: a value guesses at hosts fiki cannot know, and None reproduces the silent omission. Covering @authority by default (sibling node) binds a signature to the host it names, but the attacker controls Host, so only a list of served hosts makes a cross-service replay fail deterministically rather than depending on routing; a required decision makes every verifier confront that. Daniel ruled it on 2026-10-08 (D-B796). Tradeoff: every call site changes, including heti's src/heti/ephemeral/vanilla.py, and a service behind proxies has to learn which host names it actually receives, which is configuration it may never have written down.
        A target beginning with a slash is origin-form, whatever follows, and its authority is the Host header = decision:
          nid: 6ucuy3j5
          why: >-
            A request target handed to a verifier is read as RFC 9112 section 3.2 reads it. A target beginning with "/" is origin-form: everything before the first "?" is @path, verbatim, however many slashes it starts with, and @authority comes from the Host header. Anything else must be an absolute URI, a scheme, "://", a non-empty authority, then the path; any other shape -- "http:/x", "https:///x", "mailto:x", an authority-form "host:443", an asterisk -- is a base that cannot be built, SignatureMismatch on verify (@5zrf8gjk's rule for an unusable URL) and a caller error on sign. Chose this because fiki-py, fiki-js, fiki-go and fiki-csharp parsed "//evil.example/p" as a network-path reference, giving @authority evil.example and @path /p, so a request signed for evil.example verified at victim.example with Host: victim.example whenever the verifier passed the target as Node's req.url or Go's r.URL.String() hand it over, which is how the guide says to pass it (review A1, B1); fiki-rs and fiki-java already refused. RFC 9112's absolute-path is 1*( "/" segment ) and a segment may be empty, so "//evil.example/p" is a valid origin-form path, and reading it as anything else lets the sender choose the authority. Rejected refusing every target that begins "//", which is simpler but refuses legitimate requests to a path with an empty first segment. The absolute-form case, "https://evil.example/p" sent to victim, stays accepted as evil.example: RFC 9112 section 3.2.2 makes the target's authority authoritative over Host there, and authorities is the defence, now a required decision.
        RFC 8941 strings and whitespace are parsed to the grammar in every port = decision:
          nid: 2ejh3jdl
          why: >-
            Every port's RFC 8941 parser holds to the grammar exactly where fiki-js and fiki-go did not. An sf-string carries only %x20-21 / %x23-5B / %x5D-7E and the two escapes \" and \\, so a tab, CR, LF, NUL, DEL or any non-ASCII character inside one is MalformedSignatureInput (or the header's own malformed error), not accepted (review A2, T1: fiki-js accepted all of them, so a keyid of "E\r\nkeri" reached the resolver and came back as verdict.aid). Optional whitespace inside an inner list and after a parameter's semicolon is SP only, never HTAB, and so is the whitespace a top-level list or dictionary may skip at its start (review A5: fiki-js and fiki-go accepted a tab, py, rust and csharp refused). Chose the grammar over the leniency because the shared vectors exist so that a header one port accepts is one every port accepts (@5zrf8gjk), and a 6,000-case differential run found these two divergences that no vector pinned. Tradeoff: none known for well-formed senders, since RFC 8941 serializers never emit either.
          children:
            ? Every port runs the httpwg structured-field-tests corpus, vendored at a pinned commit, against its RFC 8941 parser = decision
            : nid: 7fexwu3s
              why: >-
                Tick 7xbw, from the 2026-10-08 review (T1; private: bakobo/reviews fiki/2026-10-08-input-hardening/testability-hawk.md): five hand-written RFC 8941 parsers had no test beyond fiki's own vectors, which is how fiki-js's lenient strings (A2) and tabs (A5) shipped. The httpwg working group's corpus is the field's shared oracle, written by the RFC's authors and used by the reference parsers, so it is independent of fiki in the way @5gf6r08f asks of a vector's expected values. It is vendored under vectors/third_party/structured-field-tests at a pinned commit, BSD-3-Clause with its LICENSE.md kept, rather than fetched in CI, so that a run never depends on the network and an upstream change arrives as a reviewed diff. It is not a fiki vector and carries no format number: a port that disagrees with it has a parser bug, not a contract to renegotiate. RFC 9421 references RFC 8941, not RFC 9651, so every port refuses Dates and Display Strings, and its runner expects that refusal for the corpus cases that use them rather than skipping them silently. Each port runs only the parts its parser exposes: dictionary, list and item parsing, and serialisation where the port serializes. Rejected fetching the corpus at test time, which makes CI flaky and lets an upstream edit change a result unreviewed. Rejected a git submodule, which every one of six toolchains' CI would have to initialize. Tradeoff: 1.3 MB of third-party JSON in the tree, refreshed by hand.
        Every port accepts exactly the signatures the cofactorless Ed25519 equation accepts = decision:
          nid: 5r75ff4m
          why: >-
            fiki pins the cofactorless Ed25519 check, [S]B = R + [k]A'. RFC 8032 section 5.1.7 makes the cofactored equation [8][S]B = [8]R + [8][k]A' the check and says it is "sufficient, but not required, to instead check" the cofactorless one, so either is conformant and fiki had never chosen: BouncyCastle in fiki-csharp checks the cofactored equation and the other five ports' libraries the cofactorless one, so for a key with a small-order component C# accepted 24 of 24 crafted signatures where rust and java accepted 3 (review B3). Only the key holder can make such signatures, so this is not a forgery, but it breaks the rule that every port gives the same answer, on the signature check itself. Chose cofactorless because it is the stricter equation (anything it accepts, the cofactored one accepts, the latter being the former multiplied by eight), so it is the fail-closed direction, and because five ports get it from their libraries, so only C# changes. C# accepts only when BouncyCastle's verification AND a hand-written cofactorless equation both pass: the conjunction accepts exactly the cofactorless set, and because BouncyCastle's check must still pass, a defect in the hand-written half can cause a false refusal but never a forgery. Rejected having every port refuse an A or R outside the prime-order subgroup, which makes the equations agree everywhere but puts hand-written curve arithmetic into six ports rather than one and costs a full scalar multiplication per verification in pure Python. Rejected the cofactored equation (ZIP-215's choice, which matters for batch verification) because fiki does not batch and five ports would have to change. Daniel ruled it on 2026-10-09 (D-SFP8). Pinned by vectors built from mixed-order signatures.
        Host is validated like any authority, keeps its port, a fragment is refused, and authorities match exactly = decision:
          nid: 7ud6x9mp
          why: >-
            Four refinements of the origin-form and authorities rules, decided by the assistant as two-way doors inside the unreleased format 3 after a fresh-context challenge of its test plan (findings X3-1, X3-2, X3-4, X2-7), each cheap to reverse before format 3 ships. HOST IS VALIDATED LIKE ANY AUTHORITY: when the Host header supplies an origin-form request's @authority, it passes every check an absolute URL's authority does -- a port that is a number from 0 to 65535, an IP-literal holding an IPv6 address or IPvFuture, no userinfo, no list of hosts, nothing but a port after the host -- or the base cannot be built and the request is SignatureMismatch (@5zrf8gjk), because rule 3 has just made Host the authority source for every origin-form request, the guide's main server path, and fiki-py was taking it verbatim, so "api.example.com:65536" and "victim.example, evil.example" verified. A HOST'S PORT IS KEPT: with no scheme no port is a default port, so Host "api.example.com:443" is @authority "api.example.com:443" and does not verify a signature over "api.example.com"; this was already fiki's rule (signature-base case relative-url-takes-authority-from-the-host-header) and is now pinned on the verify side. A FRAGMENT IS REFUSED: a request target has no fragment (RFC 9112 section 3.2), so a "#" in a received target is a base that cannot be built, SignatureMismatch, and in a URL handed to a signer it is a caller error; rejected stripping it, which fiki-py did, because a component one port strips and another keeps is exactly a divergence. AUTHORITIES ARE COMPARED EXACTLY against @authority as fiki derives it (lowercase host, a default port dropped only from an absolute URL): an entry "API.EXAMPLE.COM" or "api.example.com:443" never matches, which fails closed, and the guide says to write entries in that form. Rejected normalizing entries, which would make fiki guess what a caller meant by an entry it could instead have written exactly.
        Each port's spelling of the default minimum, its opt-out, and the authorities decision = decision:
          nid: 3cupzqtx
          why: >-
            Each port spells the two new verifier decisions in its own idiom, chosen by the port's worker against one brief and recorded here so the guide and future ports have one place to read them. THE DEFAULT MINIMUM is exported as DEFAULT_MINIMUM (Go DefaultMinimum, C# HttpSignatures.DefaultMinimum, Java Fiki.DEFAULT_MINIMUM) and applies when request verification is given no minimum; the explicit opt-out is py minimum=None, js minimum: null (undefined or absent means the default), Go NoMinimum: true (nil Minimum means the default; both set is ErrInvalidOptions), Rust Minimum::Off of enum Minimum { Default, Off, Of(Vec<String>) }, Java .withoutMinimum(), C# .WithoutMinimum(). AUTHORITIES is required on request verification: py authorities={...} or None (keyword with no default); js authorities: [...] or null, absent or undefined a TypeError, a String object refused as well as a string; Go Authorities []string or AnyAuthority: true, neither or both ErrInvalidOptions; Rust Authorities::served([...]) or Authorities::Unchecked of enum Authorities { Unstated, Unchecked, Served(BTreeSet<String>) }, Unstated refused at runtime because ..Default::default() cannot make a field mandatory; Java .withAuthorities(Collection) or .withoutAuthorityCheck(); C# .WithAuthorities(IEnumerable<string>) or .DecliningAuthorityCheck(), with a WithAuthorities(string) overload marked obsolete-as-error so a single string does not compile. Where a language's types make a misuse.json case inexpressible (a string or a number in a Go []string, a Rust BTreeSet<String>, a Java Collection<String>, a C# IEnumerable<string>), that port's driver asserts the value cannot be built into the option type, or reaches the refusal by reflection, rather than skipping the case. Response verification takes no authorities; its own default minimum and required expected_keyid are the sibling node 'verify_response applies RESPONSE_MINIMUM by default' (format 3 part two), which supersedes this node's first-draft sentence that response verification was unchanged. Chose per-port idioms over one cross-language spelling because @5zrf8gjk already made caller errors idiomatic per port, and a Go caller cannot be made to pass a required field the way a Python caller can. Tradeoff: six spellings to document, which the guide's tables carry.
        verify_response applies RESPONSE_MINIMUM by default, and expected_keyid is a required decision = decision:
          nid: 50x8vdnj
          why: >-
            verify_response fails closed by default, as verify_request does (@524c8qgv's first child): left without a minimum it applies RESPONSE_MINIMUM -- @status and the request-bound @method, @path and @query, plus content-digest when the response has a body and "content-digest";req when the request had one -- and with it everything any minimum brings (created required, keyid required, the profile's refusal order); minimum=None, in each port's idiom, is the explicit opt-out. expected_keyid becomes a required decision, as authorities is for requests: the AID the client believes it is talking to, or an explicit decline, never left unstated. Daniel ruled it on 2026-10-09 (Q-DCQK) on the reasoning that a client verifying a response is a verifier like any other, and the 0.8 default handed it no coverage policy and no identity check unless it asked: a response signed by any key at all, for any request, verified. Chose RESPONSE_MINIMUM rather than a response analogue of DEFAULT_MINIMUM with @authority, because the profile binds a response to its request through the req parameter, and the request's own authority is the client's to have checked when it sent it. Tradeoff: every client call site changes, and a response verifier must now be handed the request it answers, since the default minimum reads req-bound components.
        Every untrusted value fiki reads is bounded at 8192 bytes, and errors quote at most 64 characters of one = decision:
          nid: 3ey7xx8e
          why: >-
            Every input fiki reads is bounded before it is read, not only the three signature headers (@5zrf8gjk): a target URL and every covered field value -- Host when it supplies @authority, and any header named in the covered list -- is at most MAX_FIELD_BYTES, 8192 bytes, and over it the base cannot be built: a URL is SignatureMismatch on verify and a caller error on sign, as every unreadable URL is (@5zrf8gjk), and a field value is SignatureMismatch on either side, as fiki's existing refusal of a control character in one already is -- the first draft of this node said caller error for both, which fiki-py did not do and the part-two challenge caught. An error message quotes an untrusted value at most 64 characters long, with a marker that it was cut, and with control characters escaped. Chose this after review A9 and B9 found the URL, Host and covered values unbounded: processing stayed linear, so there was no denial of service, but a 5 MB URL produced a 10 MB Rust error carrying the whole URL twice, and fiki-js echoed control characters raw into messages a server may log. Chose 8192 because it is the bound the three signature headers already carry, so one constant bounds every untrusted field; rejected a separate bound on the number of header fields, because fiki reads only the fields it covers plus Host, Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding, and refusing a request for carrying many unrelated headers would refuse honest traffic behind proxies for no protection. Decided by the assistant as a two-way door inside unreleased format 3. Tradeoff: a legitimate URL longer than 8192 bytes cannot be verified; RFC 9112 section 3 recommends supporting request lines of at least 8000 octets, so the bound sits just above what servers are told to expect.
        ? Userinfo is refused, field names fold ASCII only, created and expires are non-negative, and the signer is pinned = decision
        : nid: 2st93fjg
          why: >-
            Three small rules that the 2026-10-08 review found unpinned and the ports had answered differently, decided by the assistant as two-way doors inside unreleased format 3. USERINFO IS REFUSED: an absolute URL whose authority carries "user@" is a base that cannot be built, SignatureMismatch on verify and a caller error on sign, because RFC 9110 section 4.2.4 says a recipient "SHOULD parse for userinfo and treat its presence as an error; it is likely being used to obscure the authority for the sake of phishing attacks", and fiki-py was stripping it silently. FIELD NAMES ARE LOWERCASED AS ASCII ONLY: a header name is folded A-Z to a-z and nothing else, so U+212A KELVIN SIGN stays itself and never becomes the "k" of a covered name (review A6, B5; py and csharp folded it, java and rust did not). CREATED AND EXPIRES ARE NON-NEGATIVE: a negative value is MalformedSignatureInput before the signature is examined, where fiki-py reported it only as a SignatureMismatch. Pinned without change, because every port already agrees or the RFC decides: http's default port 80 is dropped as https's 443 is; a scheme is case-insensitive; an empty path is "/"; a created after 2038 is an ordinary integer; tag is serialized like nonce; duplicate keys in a dictionary or parameters keep the last occurrence (RFC 8941 sections 4.2.2 and 4.2.3.2); parameters are verified in the signer's order, never re-emitted in fiki's (RFC 9421 section 2.3); field names arrive in any case; and a far expires never replaces a verifier's max_age (@67shl6c5). Every signer is pinned byte for byte by a new vectors/signs.json, since no shared vector had called a signer and a port whose default covered set dropped @query passed everything (review V-C4).
        ? 'Five part-two refinements: Content-Length past 18 digits is a body, an empty expected_keyid is a caller error, and error messages are checked by every driver = decision'
        : nid: 2n48xt4x
          why: >-
            Five refinements decided by the assistant as two-way doors inside unreleased format 3, after a fresh-context challenge of the part-two test plan. A CONTENT-LENGTH OF MORE THAN 18 SIGNIFICANT DIGITS IS A BODY: leading zeros are stripped first, and a length that cannot fit a signed 64-bit integer is read as announcing a body rather than parsed, because a port that fails to parse it and reads the failure as no body fails open, and fiki-py raised Python's own ValueError past 4300 digits on untrusted input. AN EMPTY EXPECTED_KEYID IS A CALLER ERROR: "" names no AID, and reading it as the decline, which is natural in JavaScript, would turn a caller's missing value into "accept any signer". A RESPONSE VERIFIED UNDER THE DEFAULT WITH NO REQUEST is MissingComponent, the existing refusal for a req component with nothing to read it from; rejected making it a caller error, since a minimum given explicitly already answers that way and the two should not differ. SIGN_RESPONSE WITHOUT A REQUEST is pinned rather than refused: it covers only the response's own components, which the default verifier then refuses, and the guide says so; rejected refusing at signing, which would forbid a server that genuinely signs an unsolicited message. ERROR MESSAGES are checked by every port's vector driver to hold no control character and to be at most 1024 characters, so the 64-character quoting cap is pinned portably rather than by six port-local tests.
        Each port's spelling of the response keyid decision = decision:
          nid: 6898n9xs
          why: >-
            Each port spells the response verifier's keyid decision in the idiom it already used for authorities, chosen by the port's worker against one brief: py expected_keyid=AID or None, a keyword with no default; js expectedKeyid: AID or null, absent or undefined a TypeError; Go ExpectedKeyid plus AnyKeyid: true, neither or both ErrInvalidOptions, and an empty ExpectedKeyid read as no decision because Go cannot tell it from unset; Rust ExpectedKeyid::{Unstated, Unchecked, Is(String)} with ExpectedKeyid::is(aid), Unstated refused at runtime by verify_response and read as Unchecked by verify_request, mirroring py's optional request argument; Java .withExpectedKeyid(aid) or .withoutKeyidCheck() over a Fiki.ExpectedKeyid value type; C# .WithExpectedKeyId(aid) or .DecliningKeyidCheck(). Every port refuses an empty AID as a caller error, and keeps expected_keyid optional on request verification. The response minimum's opt-out is each port's existing request opt-out. Two public API changes ride along and belong in the release notes: Rust's to_aid now returns Result and Java's toAid throws IllegalArgumentException for a key that is not 32 bytes, where they panicked or silently truncated (review B8); and Java's VerifyOptions.expectedKeyid() returns ExpectedKeyid rather than String.
